C#JavaKotlinTypeScript <p>Dispose the subscription. This cancels the request or the wait between polls.</p> <pre><code>using var subscription = events.GetObservable()
    .Subscribe(x =&gt; Console.WriteLine(x.Type));

// ...

subscription.Dispose();
</code></pre> <p>Dispose the subscription:</p> <pre><code>Disposable subscription = events.getObservable()
    .subscribe(x -&gt; System.out.println(x.getType()));

// ...

subscription.dispose();
</code></pre> <p>Dispose the subscription:</p> <pre><code>val subscription = events.getObservable()
    .subscribe { println(it.type) }

// ...

subscription.dispose()
</code></pre> <p><code>break</code> out of the loop. To stop a stream from somewhere else, pass an <code>AbortSignal</code> to <code>stream()</code>. For a streaming collection, the signal is the second argument, after the start index:</p> <pre><code>const controller = new AbortController();
setTimeout(() =&gt; controller.abort(), 60_000);

for await (const x of events.stream(controller.signal)) {
    console.log(x.type);
}
</code></pre> <p>Aborting ends the loop normally rather than throwing. A polling endpoint is the exception while a request is in flight: aborting it then throws the <code>AbortError</code> from <code>fetch</code>.</p>

C#JavaKotlinTypeScript <pre><code>var status = new PollingEndpoint&lt;Status&gt;(client, "status")
{
    PollingInterval = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)
};

// Poll for state changes
IObservable&lt;Status&gt; stream = status.GetObservable();
stream.Subscribe(x =&gt; Console.WriteLine($"Status: {x.State}"));

// Use as regular element endpoint
Status current = await status.ReadAsync();
await status.SetAsync(new Status { State = "active" });
</code></pre> <pre><code>PollingEndpoint&lt;Status&gt; status = new PollingEndpointImpl&lt;&gt;(client, "status", Status.class, null); // no end condition
status.setPollingInterval(Duration.ofSeconds(5));

// Poll for state changes
Observable&lt;Status&gt; stream = status.getObservable();
stream.subscribe(x -&gt; System.out.println("Status: " + x.getState()));

// Use as regular element endpoint
Status current = status.read();
status.set(new Status("active"));
</code></pre> <pre><code>val status = PollingEndpointImpl(client, "status", Status::class.java)
    .apply { pollingInterval = Duration.ofSeconds(5) }

// Poll for state changes
val stream = status.getObservable()
stream.subscribe { x -&gt; println("Status: ${x.state}") }

// Use as regular element endpoint
val current = status.read()
status.set(Status("active"))
</code></pre> <pre><code>const status = new PollingEndpoint&lt;Status&gt;(client, "status");
status.pollingInterval = 5000;

// Use as regular element endpoint
const current = await status.read();
await status.set({ state: "active" });

// Poll for state changes
for await (const x of status.stream()) {
    console.log(`Status: ${x.state}`);
}
</code></pre> <p>The loop keeps polling until you <code>break</code> out of it or the <code>AbortSignal</code> passed to <code>stream()</code> is triggered (see Stopping a stream).</p>

